{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the life of Sarah was a hundred years, etc.</b> Regarding Abraham it is said, ״אֵלֶּה שְׁנֵי חַיֵּי אַבְרָהָם אֲשֶׁר חָי״ (Genesis 25:7) — it adds the words ״אֲשֶׁר חָי,״ for he was a man alive in the full sense many times over in the knowledge of HaShem all the days of his life, for at three years old he recognized his Creator; which is not so of Ishmael, who did repentance only in his later days. But regarding Sarah it does not say ״אֲשֶׁר חָיְתָה,״ because a woman has the pain of childbirth and pregnancy, and the authority of her husband is upon her, and not all her days are called life. And there are those who say that Abraham did not live all the years allotted to him, for five years were taken from his days so that he would not see Esau going out into a bad way of life; therefore it says ״אֲשֶׁר חָי״ — for those years he did live, which implies that there were other days that should have belonged to him according to his nature and he did not live them, because his days were shortened as stated." "And as for what is said with the large number — 'year' (שָׁנָה) in the singular: 'a hundred year (שָׁנָה) and twenty year (שָׁנָה)' — whereas with the small number it says 'years' (שָׁנִים) in the plural: 'and seven years (שָׁנִים)' — this is because the pious, even though all their days are whole, nevertheless acquire greater perfection in their final years, the ones closest to the gates of death, than in the earlier days — whether because the elders among the sages add wisdom, or because then they go and draw ever closer to the eternal light. Therefore all the earlier years are reckoned as a single year in comparison with the later years, because on account of the abundance of perfection acquired in the later ones, those years too seem as though they were many. Hence this expression is used also regarding Abraham and Isaac, and regarding Ishmael it fits better than all of them, for he did repentance at the end of his days." "Another thing: because the later days are days of pain, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 12:1), ״וְהִגִּיעוּ שָׁנִים אֲשֶׁר תֹּאמַר אֵין לִי בָהֶם חֵפֶץ,״ therefore the later years are called by the plural — שָׁנִים — because they are days of pain. But the earlier years are likened to a few days, in His love for them."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:19.828411963Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}